welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn woke your host and our guest is  Susan hide assistant professor of  political science and international  studies at yale university professor  hides research interests include  international influences on domestic  politics elections in developing  countries international norm creation  and the use of natural and field  experimental research methods her  current research explores the effects of  international democracy promotion  efforts with a particular focus on  international election observation she  is served as an international observer  with a Carter Center and the  Organization for Security and  Cooperation in Europe for elections in  Albania Indonesia Nicaragua and  Venezuela and has also worked for the  democracy program at the Carter Center  today we'll be talking with her about  her newest endeavor the pseudo Democrats  dilemma why election monitoring became  an international norm welcome professor  Hyde thanks for having me tell us about  the premise of your book so when  describing the project I like to  describe the puzzle that I began with  and that is that I noticed that a number  of leaders throughout the world were  inviting international election monitors  and then committing large-scale election  fraud in front of them and so the book  was motivated by my interested in that  phenomenon and trying to explain it but  really what the book has become about is  why election monitoring became an  international norm okay the term pseudo  Democrat mom is that something you kind  and what actually is it I I don't think  I don't think I can claim making up the  term there's a lot of ways in which  leaders in the developing world are  described but I use it in the sense that  pseudo Democrats are leaders who are  willing to hold elections their  governments are willing to hold  elections maybe even willing to hold  democratic  but what they're not willing to do is  lose and peacefully transfer power to to  another party so they're the types of  governments throughout the world that  engage in an election manipulation  sometimes they engage in what I call  strategic manipulation which they're  trying to steal the elections but not  get caught okay and they're very  interesting leaders to study I would say  there are lots of good stories about  them okay one of the contributions of  your book is your theory of  international norm creation tell us  about it okay so it again was developed  to explain election monitoring but I now  think that it applies to other types of  international norms I think one way to  look at international politics is to  divide the world into states that are  viewed as good types of states and  states they're viewed as bad types of  states and my theory basically looks at  what happens when the preferences of  powerful states change in regard to what  types of states they prefer to work with  and so in the 1960s and then really at  the end of the Cold War in the late  1980s many powerful states began  promoting democracy with with and  expressing their preference to work with  other democratic states states  throughout the developing world  responded to this by inviting election  monitors I think they could have done a  lot of other things but they happen to  start doing this and these were these  were governments who were actually  democratizing they were looking for a  way to signal their type that they were  one of the good governments committed to  democracy to international audiences and  it became a perception that all good  types invited election monitors which  meant that if if you weren't inviting  international election monitors you must  be a bad type which which is why I think  that the behavior of inviting  international lecture monitor spread to  even those governments that are holding  fraudulent elections okay so it's kind  of it's pretty dynamic yeah so let's  talk some specific about the the  countries that you have observed so my  project focus is on the global diffusion  of election monitoring so it's  throughout the entire world  but I personally have the had the  opportunity to travel to 25 different  countries to serve as an election  monitor with three different groups and  so the first one first place I went was  Venezuela and during the recall  referendum for the Chavez and the most  recent place that I went to is Pakistan  for the 2008 elections there well that  must have been fascinating talk a little  bit in some highlights of what that was  like well that particular case was  interesting in part because the security  situation was somewhat tenuous so we  weren't really allowed to observe in a  lot of the places that I think many  people in the delegation would have  liked to have gone to but it one of the  remarkable things about the election is  that despite all the complaints and the  pre-election violence and the complaints  that many of the parties had about the  preparations for the election the  opposition actually one and this  surprised everyone at the time and it  raises a lot of questions about you know  how much do is this election  manipulation or election violence matter  if the opposition party is able to win  anyway okay let's talk a little bit  about the methodology you used in the  book how did you gather the data so I  like to call my approach as do many  people in political science right now a  multi-method approach so I have the  picture of election monitoring since it  was first initiated in sovereign states  in the early 1960s until the present but  I also use a variety of other methods  including field experimental methods and  there's a natural experiment as well and  so what that means I guess I should back  up a little bit and just say rather than  talking just about the methods one of  the important parts of my theory is that  election monitoring has to be costly to  the government's that invite election  monitors and try to steal the elections  if it doesn't matter to them then my  whole thing falls apart and it doesn't  work very well so very early on and  became interested in figuring out how  you could prove that election monitors  matter to governments are cheating and  the way that I sort of stumbled upon was  to look at micro level variation in the  performance of the cheating candidates  relative to whether international  observers were there monitoring the  polling stations or not and so the first  case was that I looked at in this way  was actually a natural experiment and  what that means is that the in this case  is that the assignment of observers to  polling stations was close to random so  everything else can be virtually held  constant and you can just compare the  group of polling station so it's  monitored with those that weren't and in  this case the incumbent did six percent  worse in the monitored polling stations  that an unmonitored polling stations  implying that he got six percent less or  fewer votes than he otherwise would have  gotten had he been allowed to or had he  not been internationally monitored in  those polling stations okay and what are  you doing when you're when you're  actually observing know is that entail  yeah so it's I think it's maybe less  interesting than it sounds at first but  there's there's a variety of types of  election monitoring but what i have done  primarily a short-term election  monitoring which is mostly around  election day and then i come in with a  bunch of other people a few days before  the election and become it a sort of  crash course and the procedures that are  supposed to be happening in that  election in that particular country in  that particular day problems that have  already happened issues to be looking  out for that sort of things so a very  intensive training period then you're  given your assignment and deployed  somewhere in the country so they try to  deploy election monitors throughout  throughout the country that that's being  observed and then during the day before  election that you meet with a lot of  party officials and election officials  police whoever is in the community that  that might have a stake in the election  see me with them the day before on  Election Day you basically just go from  polling station to point station with  the clipboard ask talking to people  filling out a questionnaire that's you  know specific to that country are the  procedures that are supposed to be  taking place actually taking place and  then  you calling your findings periodically  if there's any major problems you're  supposed to stay longer and document it  you spend a lot of time talking to  voters see if anything unusual has  happened to them trying again to  document things that are reported to you  and then usually you end up watching the  counting of the votes which can be  sometimes interesting sometimes quite  long and boring of a process and then  you sometimes will follow the the  ballots through another stage of  aggregation so they're carried up to  another level and you again watch the  tabulation process there have you been  in any countries have you observed any  elections where there has been fraud and  how can you how do you tell the fraud  has been committed oh I haven't seen  very much overt fraud with my own eyes a  lot of election monitor reports most of  them don't say this election was  fraudulent the selection was free and  fair they're much more nuanced and so  for example in Albania I didn't see a  woman cast her own ballad until well  after noon and that was because they  have a common practice they're a family  voting where the head of the household  who's always a man stands in the polling  place and cast about for every member of  this extended family is okay I mean it's  it's a traditional practice and but it's  not consistent with having a secret  ballot when the democratic election in  that sense so it wasn't a very big deal  i mean the election overall was it was  otherwise pretty good but this is a  practice that the people were quite  concerned with Venezuela things are very  tense there you spend a lot of time  getting yelled at as an international  observer in Lyonesse whele by both sides  equally but you know again you see  things that are you hear a lot more  complaints than you actually see so you  know so-and-so says that they tried to  vote and they were told that they're  actually deceased and so and so there's  a lot of a million stories like that  embedder that are harder to document  okay in your book you reach certain  conclusions talk about some of them  well one of my main conclusions I guess  a more general conclusion is that is  related to democracy promotion so a lot  of the talk about democracy promotion is  is very critical and that democracy  promotion is inconsistent for example US  democracy promotion we continue to  support dictatorship so therefore  democracy promotion policy must be  hypocritical or something like that and  what my project highlights is that that  even when other interests are at stake  for governments promoting democracy that  some some support for for democracy can  have some unintended effects and give  governments the incentive to try to at  least act like democracies if not  actually become become democratic it  also in terms of international  monitoring so this has come up recently  in relation to weapons inspection right  so the types of governments that are  there's two different states of the  world one in which if you refuse to  allow inspectors in for weapons  inspection or for international election  monitors you're assuming to be guilty  because we expect that governments have  nothing to hide will let in let in  monitors and I think an interesting part  of an implication of my book is that the  reason why election monitoring was  allowed to get to that state in which if  you don't invite election monitors your  assumed to be doing something bad was in  part because election monitoring isn't  perfect so the pseudo Democrats could  potentially fool international observers  and get a positive report for their  elections had that not happened had that  not been possible had a lecture  monitoring been perfect from the  beginning my opinion and is harder to  prove but my opinion is that that the  state leaders who are committing  election fraud just would have said we  are a sovereign state we refuse to  invite election monitors there would  have been nothing in it for them to  invite external scrutiny and I think the  same dynamic applies across a lot of  issue areas in which there is some type  of monitoring or enforcement regime okay  final question what was the most  surprising thing for  in researching and writing the book I  think I think it's the most surprising  thing but definitely one of the more fun  things is all of the methods that  governments use to steal elections so  I've had a good time just documenting  all of the the ways in which they've  been caught trying to steal elections  usually what I can find out about our  the government's are bad as stealing  elections because they're the ones who  get caught I think there's probably a  number of governments out there who are  who are actually good at concealing  election manipulation and therefore  don't get caught but I guess the other  surprise inside of this is that in that  political scientists tend to assume  assume away and they don't look at it as  an interesting question is that it's  actually quite hard to steal an election  and it's even harder to steal an  election and not get caught so there's a  lot of ways in which that can go wrong  and we've seen that we see this over and  over again so the again to bring up the  Iranians the most recent Iranian  election I think was a really  interesting case of this in which by the  end of the election day people were  pretty sure that something had gone  wrong although I think the government  came could pretty close to being able to  get away with with election manipulation  okay very entertaining thank you very  much for being here with us today and  sharing some of your research thank you  for having me for more information about  professor hide in her work please visit  our website at Yale dat edu backslash  Macmillan report be sure to join us  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at ya  you

